<Guide slug="sargassum-season" photo="tulum">
  <p>
    Sargassum is a floating brown seaweed that drifts across the Atlantic in huge rafts. Since 2011, massive blooms have
    washed up on Caribbean and Mexican beaches most years, piling up on the sand and turning the shallows brown. Recent years
    have set new records for the amount of seaweed in the Atlantic. It’s harmless in the water, but as it rots on the beach
    it smells of rotten eggs (hydrogen sulphide) and can make swimming unpleasant.
  </p>

  <h2>When is sargassum season?</h2>
  <p>
    Sargassum varies a lot from year to year and even week to week, but the pattern is consistent: arrivals usually start
    in <strong>March or April</strong>, peak from <strong>May to August</strong>, and tail off in <strong>September and
    October</strong>. Winter — December to February — is usually clear, which coincides with the dry high season.
  </p>

  <h2>Which beaches are most affected?</h2>
  <p>
    Beaches that face east into the Atlantic and the trade winds catch the most seaweed:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Mexico’s Caribbean coast:</b> <a href="/destinations/tulum/">Tulum</a>, Playa del Carmen and parts of <a href="/destinations/cancun/">Cancún</a> and the Riviera Maya can be badly hit in summer.</li>
    <li><b>Dominican Republic:</b> the east-facing beaches of <a href="/destinations/punta-cana/">Punta Cana</a>.</li>
    <li><b>Eastern Caribbean islands:</b> the Atlantic-facing coasts of <a href="/destinations/barbados/">Barbados</a>, <a href="/destinations/st-lucia/">St Lucia</a>, Guadeloupe and Martinique, plus parts of <a href="/destinations/puerto-rico/">Puerto Rico</a> and the <a href="/destinations/us-virgin-islands/">US Virgin Islands</a>.</li>
    <li><b>Also affected some years:</b> <a href="/destinations/belize/">Belize</a>, the <a href="/destinations/bahamas/">Bahamas</a>, <a href="/destinations/turks-and-caicos/">Turks and Caicos</a> and South Florida.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where to go to avoid sargassum</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>The ABC islands:</b> <a href="/destinations/aruba/">Aruba</a> and <a href="/destinations/curacao/">Curaçao</a> are rarely affected — and they’re outside the hurricane belt too.</li>
    <li><b>West-facing beaches:</b> the west coasts of Barbados, St Lucia and <a href="/destinations/cozumel/">Cozumel</a> (where most hotels are), and Isla Mujeres’ Playa Norte, are sheltered.</li>
    <li><b>The Pacific:</b> Mexico’s Pacific resorts — <a href="/destinations/cabo-san-lucas/">Los Cabos</a> and <a href="/destinations/puerto-vallarta/">Puerto Vallarta</a> — and <a href="/best-time-to-visit-hawaii/">Hawaii</a> don’t get Atlantic sargassum.</li>
    <li><b>Cenotes and islands:</b> in the Riviera Maya, swimming in cenotes is a great alternative on seaweed days.</li>
  </ul>

